# W44-118 4-mode bisection bench (initial run with A/B/C/D modes)
# Date: 2026-05-20
# Commit: pending (W44-118 ship commit on origin/main)
# Workspace: jxl-encoder--w44-118-1025469-d4-regression
# Command: W44_118_ALL_MODES=1 cargo run --release --features 'butteraugli-loop ssim2-loop parallel' --example w44_118_bisect --manifest-path jxl-encoder/Cargo.toml
#
# Modes:
#   A — JXL_W44_117_DISABLE=1  (legacy uniform-4 EPF seed, pre-W44-117 baseline)
#   B — default                (W44-117 enabled — the regression)
#   C — JXL_W44_118_DISABLE_LIFTS=1  (W44-117 on, content lifts disabled)
#   D — JXL_W44_118_PER_ITER_SHARPNESS=1  (W44-117 on, per-iter compute_epf_sharpness recompute = Option A)
#
# Cells: 8 (5 × 1025469 photo + 3 × terminal screenshot for control)
#
# Bisection findings:
# C1 (sharpness compute wrong)        — RULED OUT (Mode A restores baseline)
# C2/C4 (stale seed / placement)      — Mode D does NOT fix 1025469 AND regresses
#                                       terminal e8 d=4 SSIM2 by -3.06 (catastrophic).
#                                       Per-iter recompute is NOT a viable fix.
# C3 (content-aware lift interaction) — RULED OUT by zenanalyze probe
#                                       (JXL_W44_118_PROBE=1): 1025469 mask=76.08
#                                       in W44-91 band [50, 80) BUT fails
#                                       m3_colourfulness (45.45 < 80) AND fcbr
#                                       (0.0166 > 0.01) gates → no content-aware
#                                       lift fires on 1025469. Mode C == Mode B
#                                       byte-identical, confirming no lift fires.
#
# Conclusion: the W44-117 mechanism (one-shot iter-0 sharpness seed) is itself
# the cause. Mode D's per-iter alternative is WORSE. The right fix is to gate
# the W44-117 mechanism on `is_screenshot` (Mode F, tested in a follow-on bench
# `w44_118_mode_f_validation_2026-05-20.tsv`).
#
# Mode C is a no-op (no lift to disable on 1025469); preserved in the harness
# for future investigation when content classifiers might fire on different
# photo classes.
